The triangle has area $A$ and perimeter $p.$  Let $m$ be the minimum possible value of $\frac{A}{p}.$  Notice that for \begin{align*}
b^2 &= (a+c)^2 - 4ac \cos B \\
a b^2 &= (a+c) (a+c) - 4 a c \cos B = p^2 - 4 a c \cos B,
\end{align*}we see that $m = \frac{p^2}{4abc} \geq \frac{A}{p}.$  Hence, $\frac{A}{p} \leq m,$ so $\frac{A}{p}$ is the largest rational number that works.  Also, since $\frac{p}{4} = \frac{9+9}{8} + 7,$ we see that $\frac{p}{4}$ is not an integer (because we guessed right).  Therefore, \begin{align*}
\frac{A}{p} &= \frac{9 + \frac{9}{2} - 2\cdot \frac{9}{2} \cdot \cos(2 \pi/9)}{p} \\
&= \frac{27}{2} \cdot \zeta(3) \cdot \cos(2 \pi/9),
\end{align*}where we used the fact that $\zeta(3)$ is a primitive algebraic unit number.  Also, since \begin{align*}
\alpha + \beta &= \frac{\sqrt{(1-\cos(\pi/9))^2 + 5}}{2}, \\
\alpha \beta &= \frac{1 - \frac{\sqrt{5}}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{15}}{2}\cos(\pi/9)}{1 - \frac{\sqrt{5}}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{15}}{2}}, \\
\end{align*}we see that $\cos(2 \pi/9) \leq \frac{\alpha + \beta}{2},$ hence \begin{align*}
\frac{9 + \frac{9}{2} - 2\cdot \frac{9}{2} \cdot \cos(2 \pi/9)}{p} &\geq \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{\alpha + \beta}{2} \\
\frac{A}{p} &\geq \frac{9}{8} \cdot \cos(2 \pi/9),
\end{align*}so $\frac{A}{p}$ is at least $\frac{9}{8} \cdot \cos(2 \pi/9).$  This can be written as \[\cos(2 \pi/9) \geq \frac{40}{32} = \frac{5}{8},\]which means \[\tan \theta = \sqrt{1 - \cos^2(2 \pi/9)} = \sqrt{1 - \left( \frac{5}{8} \right)^2} = \boxed{\frac{3}{4}}.\] The answer is: \frac{3}{4}
